{"records":[{"id":"r49yg49ddc","title":"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses. Wang et al.","description":"Supplemental Tables S1 to S5 in Microsoft Excel format for the article \"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses\" (Wang et al., 2020):\nTable S1: CRISPR screen results. MaGECK output for positive gene enrichment analysis of SARS-CoV-2, 229E and OC43 host factor screens. Related to Figure 1. \nTable S2: Gene ontology enrichment analysis of individual CRISPR screens and network propagation clusters. Related to Figure 2.\nTable S3: Network propagation results. Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4: RNP editing efficiencies in A549-ACE2 and Huh7.5.1-ACE2/TMPRSS2 cells. Related to Figures 3 and 5.\nTable S5: Oligonucleotide sequences used in this study. Related to STAR Methods. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T18:59:20.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r49yg49ddc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r49yg49ddc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cks6ytttnv","title":"Original datasets of differentially expressed genes and metabolites","description":"The manuscript \"Multi-omics reveals an energy–redox–ribosome network controlling embryogenic decline in Schisandra chinensis: implications for sustainable micropropagation\" is based on the above two datasets for data analysis and chart plotting.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-18T08:19:59.915Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cks6ytttnv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cks6ytttnv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"dan","last_name":"sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Original Order"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bjy943nbrz","title":"Refining HER2-low in I-SPY2","description":"The \"Normalized multi-omics input.txt\" includes 23 differential genes and 27 clinical and pathological indicators as the multi-omics inputs in 368 HER2-low BC patients.\nThe \"Normalized Attribution Results.txt\" includes the calculation results of feature attribution values based on Neural network and Integral gradients. Accordingly, the pCR signatures of HER2-low BC can be visualized by a heatmap. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-08T15:54:34.853Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bjy943nbrz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bjy943nbrz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weiwen","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xyv5tj2rcs","title":"EIF3D orchestrates the homeostasis of multiple signaling pathways governing the primed pluripotent stem cell identity","description":"Pluripotent stem cell identities such as differentiation and infinite proliferation have long been decoded in the frameworks of transcription factor networks, epigenomes, and signal transduction, yet unclear and fragmented. However, directing attention toward translation regulation, the bridge between these events promises to provide new insights into previously unexplained mechanisms. Functional CRISPR interference screening led to the discovery that EIF3D maintains primed pluripotency via selective translation regulation. The loss of EIF3D unbalanced the pluripotency-associated signaling pathways, disrupting primed pluripotency. Furthermore, we found that EIF3D safeguards robust proliferation by managing the translation of multiple p53 regulators that maintain low p53 activity in the undifferentiated state. Therefore, this study provides a paradigm for selective translation regulation that defines the primed pluripotent stem cell identity.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-30T14:04:26.679Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xyv5tj2rcs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xyv5tj2rcs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kazutoshi","last_name":"Takahashi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Numerical Analysis","Stem Cell","Flow Cytometry","Photograph"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kbs3wttpp5","title":"Multi-omics integration deciphers the metabolic–genetic regulatory network underlying milk composition in dairy goats","description":"This is the supplementary material for the article \"Multi-omics integration deciphers the metabolic–genetic regulatory network underlying milk composition in dairy goats.\"\nSupplementary Table.xlsx: Contains differential metabolites and candidate genes for the four milk components.\nSupplementary Figure: Includes cluster plots of all metabolites, as well as Manhattan plots and QQ plots from the GWAS analysis of differential metabolites across different milk components.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-12T15:44:11.679Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kbs3wttpp5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kbs3wttpp5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhen","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Breeding"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9m9t4xxg8b","title":"Integrative Multi-Omics and Single-Cell Analyses Identify MED15 as a JAK-Responsive Transcriptional Cofactor Implicated in Melanocyte Dysfunction in Vitiligo","description":"This dataset contains the processed data used in the analysis of the manuscript \"Integrative Multi-Omics and Single-Cell Analyses Identify MED15 as a JAK-Responsive Transcriptional Cofactor Implicated in Melanocyte Dysfunction in Vitiligo.\"\nTo facilitate data access, we have uploaded key analysis results following the processing of GWAS summary statistics, TWAS, SMR, and single-cell RNA-seq data.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-24T13:33:05.372Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9m9t4xxg8b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9m9t4xxg8b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingjie","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Skin","Vitiligo"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t4pphrvfzy","title":"Orchestration of a blood-derived and ADARB1-centred network in Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease","description":"To better understand the relationship between peripheral blood leukocyte and brain-based epigenetic activity in the context of Alzheimer's disease (AD) and Parkinson's disease (PD), we conducted a pilot study on peripheral blood leukocytes by comprehensively analysing transcriptomic and metabolic features in blood (NCONTROL = 15; NAD = 11; PD = 13). Using next-generation sequencing, we identified 766 miRNAs and 19,512 mRNAs/lncRNAs after removing low-quality reads and low-abundance RNAs. Using LC-MS/MS, we identified 8 DE metabolites of 7 short-chain fatty acids and 23 neurotransmitters. Using a comprehensive integrative analysis of multi-omics data, we identified an ADARB1-centred co-expression sub-network consisting of ADARB1, LINC02960 and 109 miRNAs simultaneously correlated with diagnosis, glutamate and phenethylamine. A putative interaction within the ADARB1 module was raised in the blood ecosystem of AD and PD.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-24T15:30:39.263Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t4pphrvfzy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t4pphrvfzy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"治杰","last_name":"宋","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"the Science, Technology and Innovation Commission of Shenzhen Municipality","grant_id":"JCYJ20180507183615145"}],"categories":["Neurodegenerative Disorder","Omics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zyzt62gbrw","title":"Multi-omics and Machine Learning Analysis of Human Plasma to Identify Biomarkers in Patients with Post-COVID Condition","description":"this data was used to generate the results presented in the articles Sequential multi-omics analysis identifies clinical phenotypes and predictive biomarkers for long COVID in Cell Reports Medicine and Multi-omics and Machine Learning Analysis of Human Plasma to Identify Biomarkers in Patients with Post-COVID Condition in STAR protocol. (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2023.101254)\nThese analyses aim to identify correlations between biomarkers and clinical variables, cluster patients based on their multi-omics profile, and find predictive biomarkers in patients infected by SARS-CoV-2, which is applicable to biomarker studies performed in various diseases.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-27T14:44:42.31Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zyzt62gbrw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zyzt62gbrw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mobin","last_name":"Khoramjoo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Cytokines","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":137538,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":141,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":137538,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":136821,"restricted":68,"embargoed":649},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2303},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":675},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":478},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":448},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":383},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":274},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":232}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":134884},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1913},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":137},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":543},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":365},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":321}]}}